{"text": " friendship, financial strength, and Pearl Harbor, all covered in one conversation. If that isn't the most Trump thing you've heard, then I don't know what is. Besant responded, saying the goal was to protect the U.S. economy and keep any volatility offshore. Tokyo got asked the same question and answered in stiffer language. Japan's finance minister confirmed the action was taken in accordance with the United States Treasury and that it countered excessive volatility and disorderly movements in the Japanese yen. Translated out of Treasury speak, The yen was falling, Japan was hurting, and America had to step in and play daddy to make the pain stop. Now, maybe that's just my read. Maybe I'm connecting dots that aren't connected. Except, I'm not the only one connecting them. Mark Sobel, a guy who looks like he knows his way around an economics textbook, and who also spent about four decades inside the US Treasury working on international monetary policy, wrote that, aside from supporting a friend, the Treasury may have been concerned that a weak yen could push American long-term rates higher. So the president says friendship, the Treasury Secretary says stability, and the man who spent nearly 40 years inside the Treasury says there's more to this story. It looks like a story where the country holding more American debt than anyone else on the planet demonstrates in public that it will sell that debt to fund its own currency defense, which becomes a supply problem in a market that is at a 19-year high.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.4, "text": " friendship, financial strength, and Pearl Harbor, all covered in one conversation.", "tokens": [50363, 14738, 11, 3176, 4202, 11, 290, 18482, 21146, 11, 477, 5017, 287, 530, 5273, 13, 50599], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.176032470703125, "compression_ratio": 1.6596385542168675, "no_speech_prob": 0.02974223718047142, "words": [{"word": " friendship,", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.36, "pr